Unfortunately, if you can tolerate the message spam, you can easily never
miss with such a Monk.

More generally, Spork tries to make the middle game less of a cruise.
However, the "never miss" problem is a hard one without a radical change
to the combat dynamics - it came up in older editions of D&D, where
(because bonusses to hit are a proportion of experience level and the
proportion varies by class) at high levels you can well produce a
situation where if the magic-user can hit it, the fighter always does hit
it. Fitting chance to hit into a range from 0-20 doesn't work well when
there's such a range of potential bonusses.

It works a little better in D&D because the gamesmaster is a human and can
adjust monster ACs very carefully, but 3rd Edition patches it: if you have
+6 to hit (from class alone), you get a second attack at +1; if you have
+11 to hit, you get a second at +6 and a third at +1, etc. Hence the
high-level fighter almost certainly does hit with their first attack, but
a better attack bonus means they will hit with more of the extra attacks,
and the wizard can hit the beastie (at all). [1]

The naive response is a bell curve, but that doesn't help much because
hitting 99% of the time is not very distinguishable from hitting 100% of
the time, and getting +4 extra to hit and going to hitting 99.1% of the
time is not the most interesting thing that ever happened.

My standard proposal on the subject goes a bit like this:

Reduce the big to-hit sources except XL. Luck should scale up to maybe +5
to hit, weapon enchantment to +4.

Immediately drastically improve monster ACs. To a first approximation,
subtract each monster's HD from its previous AC. Check the list to be sure
one has not created any "holy terrors" - some ant monsters (for example)
already have good ACs early on and don't really need a boost.

However, monster AC works like player AC. If a monster has negative AC -n,
it counts as -(1 .. n), randomly selected, each time it is attacked.
However, it always gets damage reduction (1 .. n) as well.

_However_, each point by which the player's to-hit roll beats the
monster's AC cancels out one point of damage reduction. (Arguably,
monsters should get this system as well, as part of a general approach of
doing something about players' effective late-game immunity to physical
damage).

The aim of this system is to mean that additional to-hit is always
valuable, but that the player can never be completely unable to hurt a
monster. (It might totally outclass you and squash you like a bug, of
course.)

The rest of the combat proposal goes something like this:

Trim the weapons list dramatically (for example, reduce all the polearms
to "spear" (throwable), "halberd/naginata" (useful melee weapon), "pike"
(less damage, works 2 squares away).) Have the damages of the remaining
weapons more 3rd. Ed. style - the longsword isn't the only really useful
one.

"Shield use" skill, increasing the armour benefit of shields. "Tower
shield", worth base 3 AC points. Only useful combined with some "monsters
hit harder / player HP gain not unlimited" changes. If you want to be
well-protected, use a shield.

Two-handed combat multiplies skill and Strength bonuses to damage by 2. If
you are a mighty-thewed Expert and want to do a _lot_ of damage, use a
two-handed sword.

Two-weapon combat rolls to hit with both weapons. Better to-hit roll
(comparing final totals including weapon, skill etc bonuses) attacks the
monster; the other doesn't, unless the best roll was a 20/19+/18+ at
Basic/Skilled/Expert two-weapon combat. If you want to hit more, use
two-weapon; if you have a mediocre primary and an excellent secondary
(crysknife?), use two-weapon; if you have +7 Grayswandir and a +0 banana,
abandon two-weapon and concentrate on hitting people with Grayswandir.

Be able to get stuck with a cursed secondary and totally stuck with cursed
primary and secondary.

[1] If this were going to be cloned into NetHack, I would suggest that
two-weapon combat decreases the decrement between attacks from +5 to +4 or
so, but the first attack's always with the primary weapon, second with the
secondary, third with the primary, etc. The result would be that two-weapon
combat gives more attacks, but isn't the ungodly doubling-up it is at
present; it does very little if you aren't a reasonably skilled combatant;
and if you have a really good primary weapon but a terrible secondary,
you're better off without it.
